a group of graduating students in the  u.s staged a protest in honor of sherina  barclay on saturday they carried photos  of her as they were awarded their  degrees in georgetown university in  washington d.c  they're demanding an independent  investigation into her killing a  palestinian-american student carried the  palestinian flag and refused to shake  the hand of the graduation speaker  u.s secretary of state  anthony blinken  let's now talk to that student nuran  alhamdan she joins us live from  washington dc thanks for being with us  so first of all what prompted you to  make that stand  thank you so much for having me sami i  really appreciate it what prompted  myself and my peers to have this stand  is really simple  we are all graduating with a master's  degree in arab studies from georgetown  university  and we've spent the past two years  learning about uh the colonial project  in the arab world and in particular the  colonial project that is the state of  israel  i myself as a palestinian american of  course have a very intimate  understanding of what israel inflicts on  palestinians as my grandparents are  survivors of the nakbe and my parents  have you know lived the lives of  palestinian refugees so  for us uh kind of the moment we knew  that lincoln was the speaker we knew for  a fact that we were going to send him a  message um it just so happened that a  few days later unfortunately the world  was shocked with the news uh regarding  the murder of shirin  and that to us gave us a very direct uh  compass in which to point our concerns  to so we were we were you know  protesting not only the incompetent u.s  role in the region and the frankly tooth  risk toothless remarks that the united  states has in regards to someone who is  an american citizen being murdered by a  foreign army that we are supposedly  allies with and bankroll uh by the  millions and billions of dollars it was  not just a statement about shidin the  martyr but it was also a larger  statement about justice for palestinians  and and to the occupation and  palestinian liberation more generally  and given the response  and given the fact that lincoln actually  confronted us at the end of commencement  it looks like the message was heard all  right i'm glad you mentioned that as  though you had an exchange  with the u.s secretary of state tell us  what was said what happened in that  exchange  yeah this is a great question and i want  to provide some uh backgrounds maybe to  some of the viewers uh before i answer  it  myself and my peers we had planned to  don the palestinian kofiya and to hold  posters of [ __ ]  this was the best that we could do in  order to encourage  the majority of our peers to participate  because some folks had issues with  student visas or their citizens of  you know countries where free speech is  not necessarily respected and so that  was the best way for us to incorporate  everyone and  this action was seen by blinken he saw  our posters we walked into the  procession of our graduation everyone  saw the kofi as everyone saw the posters  with the martyr shooting on them and  that message was heard  now my in my own interaction with  blinken this was actually completely  unplanned uh i did not wake up that day  and think that i'm going to walk by the  secretary of state on the stage and and  speak to him we had kind of assumed that  he's supposedly an important person with  better things to do and that he would  give his speech and leave  and so i was given a palestinian flag by  an american peer of mine has no  palestinian or arab background and i  carried the palestinian flag at my  undergraduate graduation so  this just seemed like continuing a  tradition to me  something that i knew i was going to do  after lincoln spoke and we realized oh  he's on stage and he's going to stay and  we're actually expected to fist bump or  handshake him  or shake his hand  not  for myself that was an impromptu  decision that i made i